# VISUAILS — host-level redirects (Cloudflare Pages). Section 10.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The order funnel used to be six separate pages: a hub at /order and one form
# per service. Section 10 replaces all of it with a single pipeline at /start
# that asks the questions in one pass and runs them through the capacity gate.
# The old pages are deleted, so every URL below now has nowhere to land, and
# some of them are in people's inboxes, in the sitemap Google already crawled,
# and in the footer of quotes sent months ago.
#
# astro.config.mjs used to carry these as a `redirects` block. In a static
# build that emits a meta-refresh + canonical stub per route — a client-side
# redirect. It stops visitors hitting a 404, but it is not a 301: link equity
# passes murkily, the stub is a real HTML page a crawler has to fetch and
# parse, and anything that follows redirects without executing HTML (curl, a
# link checker, most crawlers that aren't Google) sees a 200 and stops. This
# file is the real answer, so the config block is gone.
#
# RULES OF THIS FILE — the two that will bite if forgotten
#
#  1. The default status code is 302, not 301. Every line below therefore
#     writes `301` explicitly. A retired route that 302s tells search engines
#     to keep the old URL indexed, which is the opposite of the point.
#
#  2. "Redirects are always followed, regardless of whether or not an asset
#     matches the incoming request." A rule here OUTRANKS a real page. So no
#     source path below may name a route the site actually serves — a typo of
#     /start or /custom-models in the left column takes that page off the air
#     with no build error and no 404 to notice it by. Check the left column
#     against `src/pages/` before adding a line.
#
# Two smaller ones, recorded so nobody re-derives them:
#
#  •  Trailing and non-trailing paths are distinct sources to Cloudflare, and
#     the site is built with `trailingSlash: 'ignore'`, so links in the wild
#     exist in both shapes. Each route is listed twice.
#
#  •  This file does not apply to requests served by Pages Functions. That is
#     fine for the destinations — a 301 to /o is a fresh request the Function
#     then answers — but it does mean a source path under /o or /api would
#     never fire. None are.
#
# No splats. `/order/* /start 301` would tidy this list and would also match
# any future route beginning /order/, silently, forever. Exact sources only:
# they are auditable against src/pages/ by eye.


# ── the retired order funnel → the pipeline ────────────────────────────────
# All five service forms and the hub collapse into one page. /start opens on
# the same first question the hub used to ask (which service), so a visitor
# arriving from a deep link to /order-video is one answer from where they were
# going, not dropped at a generic front door.
/order                  /start              301
/order/                 /start              301
/order-catalog          /start              301
/order-catalog/         /start              301
/order-custom           /start              301
/order-custom/          /start              301
/order-lifestyle        /start              301
/order-lifestyle/       /start              301
/order-video            /start              301
/order-video/           /start              301

/nl/order               /nl/start           301
/nl/order/              /nl/start           301
/nl/order-catalog       /nl/start           301
/nl/order-catalog/      /nl/start           301
/nl/order-custom        /nl/start           301
/nl/order-custom/       /nl/start           301
/nl/order-lifestyle     /nl/start           301
/nl/order-lifestyle/    /nl/start           301
/nl/order-video         /nl/start           301
/nl/order-video/        /nl/start           301


# ── order status → the client portal ───────────────────────────────────────
# /order-status was a form asking for an order reference. The portal replaces
# it: the link in the confirmation email carries the token, and /o without a
# token renders the "you need the link from your email" page rather than a
# 404 — which is exactly what someone arriving on an old bookmark needs to
# read. functions/o/index.js exists for this redirect.
#
# Both languages land on /o. There is no /nl/o: the portal is one Function
# that reads the order's own `lang` column, so it answers in the language the
# order was placed in rather than the language of the URL that reached it.
/order-status           /o                  301
/order-status/          /o                  301
/nl/order-status        /o                  301
/nl/order-status/       /o                  301


